.TH feholdexcept 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
feholdexcept \- save environment and enter non-stop mode
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.B "int feholdexcept( " fenv_t * envp " );"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBfeholdexcept()\fR function saves the current floating-point
environment (rounding mode, exception flags, and exception state) into the
object pointed to by
.BR envp .
It then clears all exception flags and installs the default rounding mode.

This establishes a clean environment for a sequence of operations. The
typical usage pattern is:
.TP
1. Call \fBfeholdexcept()\fR to save the state and clear exceptions.
.TP
2. Perform a sequence of floating-point operations.
.TP
3. Test for exceptions with \fBfetestexcept()\fR.
.TP
4. Restore the saved state (and re-raise any new exceptions) with
   \fBfeupdateenv()\fR.

On x86, this corresponds to saving the x87 control word/MXCSR and then
resetting them to the default state (FE_DFL_ENV).

.SH RETURN VALUE
The \fBfeholdexcept()\fR function returns zero on success.
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined. The function does not set
.BR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>

double safe_sqrt(double x) {
    fenv_t saved_env;

    /* Save env, clear flags, set default rounding */
    feholdexcept(&saved_env);

    double result = sqrt(x);

    /* Restore saved env and re-raise any new exceptions */
    feupdateenv(&saved_env);

    return result;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfenv\fR(7), \fBfegetenv\fR(3), \fBfeupdateenv\fR(3), \fBfetestexcept\fR(3), \fBfeclearexcept\fR(3)
